33	/**
34	 * The claims on a sheet: added to a person and edited (amount, date, note, external reference). A
35	 * claim's reference, when it has one, is unique on its sheet without regard to case. A claim given no date
36	 * takes the sheet's today. A claim starts as DRAFT and moves through its statuses (see
37	 * {@link ClaimStatus}); a change its status does not allow is refused. Only a DRAFT can be edited:
38	 * to change an open claim, void it and add a new one. Settling a claim fixes its
39	 * due at the rate its person's grade has then for the claim's date; an open claim's due stays live.
40	 * A claim records who opened it, and that user cannot settle it (four eyes). A claim bound by its
41	 * person's last statement keeps the figure it had then, and settling it fixes that figure. A claim
42	 * belongs to the period its due-by falls in (fixed at creation: an edit, re-date or move leaves it).
43	 * No claim due inside a closed period can be added, edited or change status. No
44	 * claim can be added or edited to a date after its person's retirement date. A clerk settles a claim
45	 * only when its figure is at or below the sheet's clerk limit (a claim with no figure is within any
46	 * limit); a clerk settling one above it makes it PENDING instead. A steward settles any claim, and
47	 * approves a PENDING claim (SETTLED, its due fixed then) or rejects it with a reason (back to OPEN);
48	 * the user who opened a claim cannot approve it. The user who last edited a claim (through its form or
49	 * its row's amount) can neither settle nor approve it, as its opener cannot. A PENDING claim is not bound by a statement: it
50	 * shows its live figure, and is bound again by the last statement once rejected. An overdue claim's due gains a late charge on its
51	 * figure (see {@link LateCharge}), and the floor is applied last: a claim's live due is never below
52	 * the sheet's floor. A statement binds only the rate figure, not the charge nor the floor, and settling fixes the charge into the settled due. A claim with
53	 * a negative amount is a correction: it is allowed only when it names, by number, a SETTLED claim
54	 * of the same person that it corrects. Its due has no floor and no late charge. A steward can move
55	 * a DRAFT, OPEN or PENDING claim to another person: it keeps its date, takes the next number (a
56	 * DRAFT, having none, gets none), loses any figure
57	 * a statement bound it to, and is listed as its new person's newest claim. A steward can re-settle
58	 * a SETTLED claim: its due is fixed again at the figure it would have today as an open claim bound
59	 * by no statement (today's rates, grades, floor and late charge). An OPEN claim can be deferred to
60	 * a date after the sheet's today: until the sheet's today reaches it the claim counts in no total,
61	 * is not overdue, cannot be settled and is left unbound by a statement; from then on it is an OPEN
62	 * claim like any other. A claim has a proofs count, raised by one at a time while it is not SETTLED
63	 * or VOID. An OPEN claim can be split: an amount split off it becomes a new OPEN claim (see
64	 * {@link #split}). A claim whose figure is above the proof threshold in force on its date cannot be settled
65	 * or approved while it has no proofs: that is refused before the clerk limit is considered. A claim
66	 * whose due is above the note threshold in force on its date cannot be opened while its note is empty.
67	 * Who may open, settle, void or move a claim is the sheet's permission matrix (see
68	 * {@link Sheet#permits}): an action the user's role may not take is refused (403). When a claim is
69	 * settled, approved or re-settled, each user watching its person is notified, in the order they
70	 * started watching. A steward may void a SETTLED claim within {@value #VOID_SETTLED_DAYS} days of its
71	 * settlement (the date its status log records it SETTLED) by the sheet's today: it keeps its number and
72	 * its frozen figure, which leaves every total.
73	 */
74	@Service
75	public class ClaimService {
